WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.162
- Naumis is writing in,

00:00:05.162 --> 00:00:08.430
"Diverse teams may come
with creative ideas

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.097
"alongside when you need quick consensus

00:00:11.097 --> 00:00:13.900
"among members with diverse
backgrounds and culture,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.677
"how do we achieve it so that the firm

00:00:15.677 --> 00:00:19.065
"moves forward ahead as one team?"

00:00:19.065 --> 00:00:20.739
- That's a great question.

00:00:20.739 --> 00:00:23.072
Hey Naumis, I've got this...

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.857
language is pretty important to me,

00:00:26.857 --> 00:00:28.518
I think it's really good.

00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:30.281
And the more diversity you have

00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:32.572
you tend to find different
cultures coming together.

00:00:32.572 --> 00:00:35.481
So I think you need a
common language around,

00:00:35.481 --> 00:00:36.486
how do we serve them?

00:00:36.486 --> 00:00:39.361
And number one, always use phrases like,

00:00:39.361 --> 00:00:41.399
we've got to put ourselves
in the shoes of the customer,

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:43.006
what does the customer need?

00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:45.711
Even in meetings I sometimes keep

00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:47.709
an empty chair for the customer.

00:00:47.709 --> 00:00:49.253
What would the customer want?

00:00:49.253 --> 00:00:52.781
What would the customer
want us to do here?

00:00:52.781 --> 00:00:54.537
I've always encouraged to have a culture

00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:56.686
where you get facts and data in the room.

00:00:56.686 --> 00:00:58.711
If anybody's got a point of view

00:00:58.711 --> 00:01:00.198
they should say it.

00:01:00.198 --> 00:01:03.176
And I think as a leader
you want to encourage

00:01:03.176 --> 00:01:05.318
the people who don't speak often,

00:01:05.318 --> 00:01:07.890
'cause sometimes they're the
brightest people in the room,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.557
but they might feel intimidated.

00:01:11.431 --> 00:01:12.974
And then when you've got that you frame,

00:01:12.974 --> 00:01:14.679
what is it we're trying to achieve here?

00:01:14.679 --> 00:01:16.804
What is the right answer for the customer?

00:01:16.804 --> 00:01:17.637
How do we get to mark?

00:01:17.637 --> 00:01:21.183
Or whatever the problem of the day is.

00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:24.339
And then you get agreement around

00:01:24.339 --> 00:01:26.026
what the decision is.

00:01:26.026 --> 00:01:29.129
And then a phrase I use in tur-nil-la is

00:01:29.129 --> 00:01:31.103
if you make a decision

00:01:31.103 --> 00:01:33.854
and you take no accountability, no action,

00:01:33.854 --> 00:01:38.275
and no timeline, and no outcome
of what that decision is

00:01:38.275 --> 00:01:40.881
then if you leave the
scene of that decision

00:01:40.881 --> 00:01:44.379
then consider that there
was no decision ever made.

00:01:44.379 --> 00:01:46.685
Because a decision without an outcome

00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:49.185
or an action is hallucination.

00:01:50.404 --> 00:01:51.997
It never happens.

00:01:51.997 --> 00:01:55.125
So I think a great
culture, a great culture

00:01:55.125 --> 00:01:57.275
is where you have the discussion,

00:01:57.275 --> 00:01:58.793
you get the facts and the data in,

00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:00.173
you put yourself in the
shoes of the customer,

00:02:00.173 --> 00:02:01.829
say how do we make a difference here?

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:04.303
You make the decision, you lock that down,

00:02:04.303 --> 00:02:05.598
get everybody crystal clear

00:02:05.598 --> 00:02:07.612
about what their accountability is

00:02:07.612 --> 00:02:09.510
and who's going to be responsible

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:11.920
for delivering that for the team.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.237
And it could be a team effort.

00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:15.852
And then you say, right,
what does success look like?

00:02:15.852 --> 00:02:16.685
What's the outcome?

00:02:16.685 --> 00:02:17.518
When are we going to have it,

00:02:17.518 --> 00:02:19.755
30 days, 10 days, five days?

00:02:19.755 --> 00:02:22.029
And so let's get back
together the next week,

00:02:22.029 --> 00:02:25.139
let's have a coffee and
just make sure we did

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:27.560
what we said we would.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.425
As a leader you can really
be a bit of a hustler,

00:02:31.425 --> 00:02:33.796
and also, I think where this is important,

00:02:33.796 --> 00:02:35.456
you've got to be authentic
again with yourself.

00:02:35.456 --> 00:02:37.221
You've got to hold yourself to account,

00:02:37.221 --> 00:02:39.276
so if you commit to things for the team,

00:02:39.276 --> 00:02:40.841
you've got to deliver them for you.

00:02:40.841 --> 00:02:42.549
And you've got to walk the talk

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:45.906
and set the tone for the
company you're building.

00:02:45.906 --> 00:02:48.268
- I like that empty seat for the customer.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.101
I've never heard that.

00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:50.277
I've consumed a lot of content,

00:02:50.277 --> 00:02:51.668
I've never heard anybody do that,

00:02:51.668 --> 00:02:53.916
have an empty seat and
imagine the customer's there.

00:02:53.916 --> 00:02:56.416
That's cool, I might use that.

00:02:58.165 --> 00:03:00.294
I think two things, I agree with Stephen,

00:03:00.294 --> 00:03:04.028
I think the mission is the unifying goal,

00:03:04.028 --> 00:03:05.949
like, why are we doing this?

00:03:05.949 --> 00:03:08.272
If you have clarity on why you're here,

00:03:08.272 --> 00:03:10.027
that makes it so much
easier to gather around

00:03:10.027 --> 00:03:11.904
and move forward.

00:03:11.904 --> 00:03:14.992
I think too, in terms
of cultural diversity,

00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:18.467
a lot of the work can be
done in advance as well.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:20.964
Ideally, if you're over communicating

00:03:20.964 --> 00:03:22.709
and really try to understand each other,

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:24.019
then you already have a sense

00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:26.728
of how to work together
before you come to the table

00:03:26.728 --> 00:03:28.141
to make this really important decision

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:31.264
and not have to figure
that out on the spot.

00:03:31.264 --> 00:03:33.887
If you look at Stephen,
he led off with a quote

00:03:33.887 --> 00:03:36.975
from Justin Trudeau, who is
the Canadian Prime Minister,

00:03:36.975 --> 00:03:38.788
and every time we do
something, I'm Canadian,

00:03:38.788 --> 00:03:40.866
so there's always some
Canadian content in there.

00:03:40.866 --> 00:03:42.739
I always appreciate that, right?

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:44.099
Like Stephen's done his homework.

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:45.380
He's always prepared.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.686
I'm sure when he's going to France,

00:03:46.686 --> 00:03:49.542
he has some French thing that
he's going to be sharing.

00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:51.005
And that means a lot, you know,

00:03:51.005 --> 00:03:52.230
to the people that you're talking to,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.343
that you've taken the time to understand

00:03:54.343 --> 00:03:56.015
their culture, to understand
them a little bit more,

00:03:56.015 --> 00:03:57.240
so that when you do get together

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.346
and you have that
important decision to make,

00:03:59.346 --> 00:04:01.483
it makes it easier to move
forward in the same paths.

00:04:01.483 --> 00:04:03.706
So the alignment around the goal, I know,

00:04:03.706 --> 00:04:05.455
so the prep work, the ground work,

00:04:05.455 --> 00:04:08.868
the awareness to understand them

00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:10.550
prior to having to make that decision,

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.967
I think is really important.

